translator: re-point the R8 constructor sites the plan missed

Two kinds of `new T` + `S.<init>` (S a superclass of T) were left unverifiable.
Harmless while HotSpot does not verify app classes, but the AOT training run is
the one place it does, and there they were fatal (Instagram's QPLInitializer
never finished; the app called System.exit(10)).

1. InitRepointPlan: reusing R8's surviving pure forwarder on T made it the FIXED
   caller of everything above it, so a superclass constructor that does real work
   could not be routed around and the whole site was dropped. The reuse is now
   tried as a whole and, when blocked, taken back: a padded forwarder is
   synthesised on T as well (X/01aq -> 01ap.<init>()V calls this.A02()).
2. InitRepointPlan: the linear scan pairs an allocation only with a constructor
   BELOW it; R8 lays blocks out freely, so a flow-insensitive pass now proposes
   (T, S) for every `new T` that can reach the receiver register and has S as a
   proper ancestor. Over-proposing is harmless: every re-point is still decided
   by type inference.
3. Translator.hasHiddenRepoint: one `new Object()` in a method hid every other
   Object.<init> from the exact pass. Each call now consumes one allocation of
   its class; a surplus call triggers the pass.
4. Translator: the DEX2JVM_TRACE_ENTRY prologue read each argument from the next
   one's slot (spacedParams() lists the receiver first), so a boolean `true` went
   out as the reference 0x1 and the trace itself threw.

Measured: the AOT training run reaches the window and the feed (the cache grows
from 199 to 325 MB) and the app opens in 6 s with it.

